## 1. Introduction
Kidney tissue and renal cells are required for applications in compound efficacy and safety screening, regenerative medicine, bioartificial kidneys and disease modelling. A major hurdle is the availability of functional human renal cells and tissue (Jansen et al. , 2014, Tasnim et al. , 2010, Tiong et al. , 2014). Stem cell-based approaches are attractive for addressing this problem. However, whereas approaches for the directed differentiation of stem cells into tissue-specific cell types had been well established with respect to various other organs and tissues, such as liver, heart or cartilage, nothing comparable was available for the kidney.
This situation started to change in 2012, when a protocol was established for the differentiation of human induced pluripotent stem cells (hiPSC) into renal podocyte-like cells (Song et al. , 2012). Briefly afterwards in 2013 a race started on the differentiation of pluripotent stem cells (PSC) into different renal-like cell types and precursor-like cells of the renal lineage, and various protocols were published (Araoka et al. , 2014, Kandasamy et al. , 2015, Kang and Han, 2014, Lam et al. , 2014, Mae et al. , 2013, Narayanan et al. , 2013, Taguchi et al. , 2014, Takasato et al. , 2014, Xia et al. , 2013). In parallel, first methods for applications of such cells were established (Kandasamy et al. , 2015, Li et al. , 2014, Toyohara et al. , 2015). The race continues and focuses currently on the generation of selforganizing kidney organoids (Freedman et al. , 2015, Morizane et al. , 2015, Takasato et al. , 2015). Thus, amazing progress has been made during the last four years and exciting opportunities become available. Here, we will review these exciting developments.
Before we go into the details of current PSC-based approaches, we will briefly summarize background information on the kidney and its development. The kidney is essential for the clearance of xenobiotics and metabolic waste from the body, is indispensable for volume, electrolyte and pH homeostasis, and has also endocrinologic functions (Brenner, 2008, Fraser and Kodicek, 1970, Wilson et al. , 2004). For instance, the renal interstitium contains specialized cells producing renin, which is part of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone hormone system that regulates blood volume and blood pressure. Specialized interstitial fibroblasts produce about $90\%$ of the body’s erythropoietin, which controls red blood cell production (Kaissling and Le Hir, 2008, Zeisberg and Kalluri, 2015).
The functional unit of the kidney which is essential for most other renal functions is the nephron. The average nephron number per kidney is \~ 1 million, but there are substantial individual differences (Bertram et al. , 2011). The nephron consists of the glomerulus, where filtration of the blood occurs, and the renal tubule, into which the glomerular filtrate flows (Scott and Quaggin, 2015). The renal proximal tubule (PT) is next to the glomerulus. The loop of Henle connects the PT with the distal tubule (DT), which leads into the collecting duct (CD; Fig. 1). The CD is not part of the nephron, and the branched CD tree connects the nephrons with the ureter (Brenner, 2008).
Figure 1: Human kidney and nephron structure
Glomeruli contain a tuft of capillaries lined by the glomerular filtration barrier, which consists of specialized endothelial cells, a basal lamina and the glomerular podocytes. Podocytes have a special morphology, and their foot projections wrap around the capillary walls and form a slit diaphragm, which is part of the glomerular filtration barrier (Reiser and Altintas, 2016, Scott and Quaggin, 2015).
The glomerular filtrate flows first into the PT, which is lined by the proximal tubular cells (PTC). PTC have manifold functions. For instance, they control the pH of blood and urine and reabsorb most of the water, glucose, peptides and proteins from the glomerular filtrate (Brenner, 2008, Curthoys and Moe, 2014). They are important for electrolyte and mineral homeostasis and produce the most active form of vitamin D (Brenner, 2008, Curthoys and Moe, 2014, Fraser and Kodicek, 1970). They also make major contributions to the clearance of metabolic wastes, toxicants and xenobiotics by active transport and secretion of these compounds from the bloodstream into the glomerular filtrate. This is achieved by various transporters for organic molecules, and different sets of transporters are expressed on the apical and baso-lateral sides of PTC, which are polarized epithelial cells (Anzai et al. , 2005, Lash et al. , 2006, Morrissey et al. , 2013, Morrissey et al. , 2012). In addition, PTC are active in compound metabolism and express a large variety of phase I and phase II enzymes for the metabolism of toxicants, organic compounds and drugs, such as cytochrome P450 enzymes, glutathione-S-transferases and UDP-glucuronosyltransferases (Lash et al. , 2008, Lohr et al. , 1998).
As all compounds are heavily concentrated in PT due to water reabsorption, and PTC are very active in compound transport, metabolism, clearance and reabsorption, PTC are a major target for toxic drug effects. Therefore, PTC are highly relevant for in vitro toxicology (Davies, 2013, Huang et al. , 2014b, Li et al. , 2013, Su et al. , 2015, Tiong et al. , 2014). As PTC also perform a broad variety of other renal functions that need to be replaced in kidney patients, PTC are applied in bioartificial kidneys (Humes et al. , 2004, Jansen et al. , 2014, Oo et al. , 2013, Tasnim et al. , 2010, Tumlin et al. , 2008). Furthermore, PTC are important for applications in disease models (Chintagari et al. , 2015, Huang et al. , 2014a, Vallon, 2011). Various protocols have now been established for the generation of PTC-like cells and other renal-like cell types and kidney organoids from PSC, and the current approaches will be described in the following sections. Most of the protocols aim at recapitulating key stages of renal embryonic development. Kidney development will be briefly summarized here before the PSC-based protocols are dicussed.
The kidney is derived from mesoderm during embryonic development, and the precursor of mesoderm and endoderm is the primitive streak (PS; Fig. 2), which is also referred to as mesendoderm (ME) (Arnold and Robertson, 2009, Wang and Chen, 2015). Prospective mesodermal cells ingress through the PS during gastrulation, and cells from a posterior section of the PS will give rise to the so-called intermediate mesoderm (IM). A stripe of IM is located on each side of the early embryo between the somites and the lateral plate mesoderm (Fig. 2). The IM is an early precursor of urogenital organs. The so-called metanephric mesenchyme (MM) is derived from the posterior IM and the ureteric bud (UB) is derived from the anterior IM(Takasato and Little, 2015) . The adult kidney is derived from these two structures (Davies, 2002, Takasato and Little, 2015, Vainio and Lin, 2002). The UB invades the MM and performs branching morphogenesis (Fig. 2). In this way the precursor of the UBderived CD tree is generated. At the tips of the UB branches the MM condenses and is induced to perform mesenchymal-to-epithelial transition. These MM-derived epithelial cells give rise to all epithelial cell types of the nephron, including the various types of tubular cells and the glomerular podocytes (Davies, 2002, Vainio and Lin, 2002). The following sections will describe how renal precursor-like structures and respective cell types can be generated in vitro from PSC.
## 2. Novel PSC-based protocols
2.1. PSC-based protocols for the generation of renal precursor-like structures and cells with properties of mature renal cells
Here in section 2 we will give an overview over the various protocols used for the generation of PSC-derived renal-like cells and organoids, and section 2 focuses on the methodology. A broader discussion of the methods in the light of current and potential applications is provided in section 3.
Some protocols for the directed differentiation of PSC into cells of the renal lineage have been developed during the past years, which were mainly based on murine embryonic stem cells (mESC) (Bruce et al. , 2007, Fuente Mora et al. , 2012, Kim and Dressler, 2005, Kramer et al. , 2006). One protocol used human embryonic stem cells (hESC) (Batchelder et al. , 2009). These protocols were not suitable for the efficient generation of cells of the renal lineage that could be used in relevant applications. However, the seminal study by Kim and Dressler (2005) applied a combination of retinoic acid (RA), activin A (AA) and bone morphogenetic protein (BMP)7. Based on insights into vertebrate embryonic development RA and AA were chosen in order to induce IM markers. BMP7 is essential for kidney development and regeneration and promotes formation and repair of renal tubules (Patel and Dressler, 2005, Simic and Vukicevic, 2005, Zeisberg, 2006, Zeisberg et al. , 2005). RA, AA and BMP7 are now widely applied in current protocols for the differentiation of PSC into renal-like cells and structures (Figs. 3 and 4). In addition, other BMPs and various fibroblast growth factors (FGF), which play important roles in IM and MM induction and differentiation (Carev et al. , 2008, James and Schultheiss, 2005, Takasato and Little, 2016), as well as in metanephric kidney development (Cain et al. , 2008, Motamedi et al. , 2014, Walker et al. , 2016), are widely applied in current protocols (Figs. 3 and 4).
Figure 3: Protocols for the directed differentiation of PSC into renal-like cells and precursors
A key step in many protocols is intial induction of a ME-/PS-like state, as indicated by an early and transient peak of brachyury (T). T is expressed in early embryonic ME and regulates mesoderm formation (Martin and Kimelman, 2010, Technau, 2001). Various factors used in the different protocols are known inducers of ME/PS-like states, such as AA and Wnt3a (D'Amour et al. , 2006, Gadue et al. , 2006). Also the small molecule glycogen synthase kinase $3\upbeta$ inhibitor and Wnt agonist CHIR99021 is frequently used for induction of ME-/PS-like states (Figs. 3 and 4).
All studies described here (Figs. 3 and 4) identified the various precursor-like states and cell types obtained by analysing marker expression patterns. Markers that are typically expressed in specific renal precursor structures during embryonic development and that were widely used in the in vitro protocols discussed here for identifying respective states are listed in Figure 2. Individual studies often used additional markers that are not discussed here in detail. However, it should be noted that many widely used markers, like Wilms’ tumor (WT)1 and Odd-skipped related (OSR)1, are expressed in various precursor structures throughout renal development (Kreidberg, 2010, Mugford et al. , 2008). Furthermore, OSR1 is not only expressed in the IM, but also in other mesodermal structures not related to kidney development. In contrast, WT1 is expressed only in renal precursors, but also in mature podocytes in the adult kidney (Kann et al. , 2015, Kreidberg, 2010). Other markers like paired box (PAX)2, which are widely expressed in vivo during many stages of renal development, but not in mature renal cells, become reexpressed in mature renal cells under in vitro conditions (Elberg et al. , 2008).
Therefore, the state of PSC-derived cells cannot be reliably identified by their marker expression patterns only, and it is important to test also other properties of the obtained structures and cell types. Widely applied is, for instance, the embryonic kidney dissociation/re-aggregation assay (Unbekandt and Davies, 2010). In this assay embryonic kidneys are dissociated into single-cell suspensions, which are then re-aggregated. During re-aggregation similar cells aggregate preferentially with each other and the different cell types self-organize again into renal-like structures. When mixed with in vitrodifferentiated cells, these may preferentially integrate into specific renal structures, which gives additional clues about the identity of the cells and their developmental potential.
In the kidney dissociation/re-aggregation assay, IM-like cells obtained by Xia et al. (2013 and 2014) integrated into and contributed to the development of UB-derived structures, which revealed that the IM-like cells obtained by this protocol were UB-committed (Xia et al. , 2013, Xia et al. , 2014) (Fig. 3). The generation of UB-committed cells is a unique feature of this protocol, as cells obtained with other current protocols for the generation of specific renal-like cell types display features typical for cells of the nephron (Fig. 3). For downstream applications further maturation of the UB-committed cells would be required, which can be achieved in chimeric cultures with embryonic mouse kidney cells (Xia et al. , 2013, Xia et al. , 2014). The dependency on embryonic mouse kidney cells and chimeric cultures is a potential limitation of this method, especially with respect to cost-effectiveness, animal-free screening methods and applications in regenerative medicine.
Other protocols for the generation of IM-like cells (not UB-committed) were developed by Mae et al. (2013; Fig. 3). Such IM-like cells expressed OSR1 and other IM markers. These cells could be further differentiated in vivo and in vitro into gonadal- and adrenocortical-like cells and cells with properties of more mature renal cell types, in agreement with the fact that the IM is the early embryonic precursor of urogenital and adrenocortical tissues. More differentiated cell types were only obtained at low frequencies, which would not be sufficient for any in vitro application, such as compound safety screening (Mae et al. , 2013).
Based on this approach a much shorter protocol for the generation of IM-like cells was then established. Uniquely, this method was only based on small molecules (CHIR99021, AM580 and TTNPB; Fig. 3) added to the cell culture media (Araoka et al. , 2014). AM580 and TTNPB are retinoic acid receptor agonists, which were identified based on their property to induce OSR1 expression in AA and CHIR99021-treated cells. Use of small molecules is an advantage in terms of cost-effectiveness, reproducibility and potential applications in regenerative medicine. Interestingly, the protocol by Araoka et al. (2014) is the only current protocol where T was only induced with low frequency. The authors concluded that ME induction can be bypassed, which is surprising, as ME induction is a key step in most other protocols. By bypassing ME induction IM-like cells could be obtained within a relatively short time period by Araoka et al. (2014). However, although the protocol was shorter, the same problem applied here as in case of Mae et al. (2013), in a sense that mainly early precursor cells had been obtained, which would not be suitable for downstream applications like compound screening.
Besides protocols for the generation of IM-like structures, also various protocols were developed for the directed differentiation of PSC into cells with properties of more mature renal cell types, as required for many applications. Song et al. (2012; Fig. 3)
achieved differentiation of hiPSC into podocyte-like cells within 10 days. These cells had a morphology resembling podocytes, expressed the podocyte markers podocin and synaptopodin, and displayed functional characteristics of podocytes such as a contractile response to angiotensin II. The cells also expressed WT1 and integrated into WT1- positive regions in the embryonic kidney re-aggregation assay (Song et al. , 2012). WT1 is essential for kidney development and is expressed throughout kidney development (Kreidberg, 2010). It remains to be expressed in the podocytes of the adult kidney, whereas it is silenced in other fully differentiated renal cell types (Kann et al. , 2015).
Also Kang and Han (2014) have published a protocol for the generation of podocyte-like cells, and a variant of this protocol generated renal tubular-like cells. These protocolscovered extensive time periods of up to ${\sim}50$ days (Kang and Han, 2014) (Fig. 3). Recently, also other robust protocols for generating podocytes have been established (Ciampi et al. , 2016, Nishinakamura et al. , 2016), and the progress in this area will help to obtain reliable sources of podocytes for disease modelling and regenerative therapies.
Other approaches have focused on the generation of PTC-like cells. For instance, Lam et al. (2014; Fig. 3) have developed protocols for the generation of PTC-like cells from hESC or hiPSC. With one of these protocols, PTC-like cells could be obtained within \~11-12 days (the differentiation time required by the various protocols is counted in this review consistently from the time point of cell seeding). Similar to most other protocols, also this protocol was based on stepwise induction recapitulating developmental stages. Remarkably, during the last step of differentiation only renal cell medium was applied, that was not supplemented with any additional growth factors (Fig. 3). This suggested that renal cell medium was sufficient for promoting differentiation into PTC-like cells after induction of IM-like structures. When cells derived by this protocol were implanted beneath the kidney capsule of immunodeficient mice, they generated human growths that expressed the water transporter aquaporin (APQ)1, which is expressed in the kidney only by PTC (Lam et al. , 2014). A variant of this protocol (Fig. 3) used FGF9 and AA to induce expression of the nephron precursor marker SIX homeobox (SIX)2, and these cells could be further induced with CHIR99021 to express a PTC marker. These cells formed clusters of cells expressing a PTC marker in re-aggregates with dissociated mouse embryoic kidneys (Lam et al. , 2014).
An even more rapid and simple protocol was published by Kandasamy et al. (2015), which comprised only 8 days and one step. This hiPSC-based protocol generates socalled HPTC-like cells, which under in vitro conditions are similar to human primary renal proximal tubular cells (HPTC). A detailed characterization of functional features and of the expression of transporters, enzymes and other markers in hESC- and hiPSCderived HPTC-like cells has been performed by qPCR, immunostaining and functional assays in comparison to HPTC (Kandasamy et al. , 2015, Narayanan et al. , 2013). Overall, comparable results have been obtained with HPTC and HPTC-like cells.
In general, the expression of transporters and drug metabolizing enzymes is different in vitro compared to the in vivo levels. This applies to all available cell models (PSCderived HPTC-like cells, permanently or conditionally immortalized PTC and primary cells (HPTC)). There is a general trend towards down-regulation, although every cell model is different in its own ways. Detailed results and discussions on this have been provided in previous studies (Jenkinson et al. , 2012, Kandasamy et al. , 2015, Tiong et al. , 2014, Weiland et al. , 2007). As mentioned above, hiPSC-derived HPTC-like cells are comparable to primary human PTC (HPTC). To our knowledge, the articles by Kandasamy et al. (2015) and Narayanan et al. (2013) are the only publications on PSCderived renal cells that show comprehensive qualitative and quantitative data on the expression and function of transporters and drug metabolizing enzymes.
In contrast to all other protocols discussed, so far, the protocol developed by Kandasamy et al. (2015) involved only one single step, which was application of renal epithelial cell growth medium (REGM) supplemented with BMP2 and BMP7 on day one. Intriguingly, although different steps for recapitulating the various stages of renal embryonic development were not applied, the cells proceeded through similar changes of gene expression patterns during the differentiation period as observed with respect to most multi-step protocols discussed here: transient up-regulation of T, followed by induction of OSR1 and renal precursor markers (Kandasamy et al. , 2015).
This short hiPSC-based protocol was developed on the basis of the longer 1-step protocol published by Narayanan et al. (2013) that was based on hESC (Fig. 3). hESC-derived HPTC-like cells had been shown to integrate into renal tubular epithelia when injected into ex vivo cultivated murine embryonic kidneys, and formed AQP1-expressing tubules when implanted into immundeficient mice (Narayanan et al. , 2013). Both, hESC-derived HPTC-like cells and hiPSC-derived HPTC-like cells were applied successfully for the development of in vitro models that predict PTC toxicity in humans with high accuracy (Kandasamy et al. , 2015, Li et al. , 2014). These are the first and currently only stem cellbased predictive renal models. Applications in in vitro toxicology will be discussed in detail below (3.1). It should be noted that marker expression and sub-cellular localization in HPTC-like cells is influenced by the hiPSC line used, and not all hiPSC lines give satisfactory results. Comparisons of results obtained with three different hiPSC lines are shown in Kandasamy et al. (2015).
2.2. PSC-based protocols for the generation of complex renal-like structures and selforganizing kidney organoids
A very recent development in the field is the generation of self-organizing kidney tissue-like structures and kidney organoids. In fact, whereas impressive progress had been made in the field of self-organizing stem cell-derived organoids during the last decade, and conditions for the generation and culture of gut organoids, retinal organoids and brain and liver organoids had been established (Eiraku et al. , 2011, Eiraku et al. , 2008, Lancaster and Knoblich, 2014, Lancaster et al. , 2013, Nakano et al. , 2012, Ranga et al. , 2014, Sato et al. , 2009, Spence et al. , 2011, Takebe et al. , 2013), nothing comparable was available with respect to the kidney. This situation has changed, and three different groups have published recently protocols for generating PSC-derived self-organizing kidney tissue-like structures and kidney organoids in vitro (Freedman et al. , 2015, Morizane et al. , 2015, Taguchi et al. , 2014, Takasato et al. , 2014, Takasato et al. , 2016, Takasato et al. , 2015). This area is now of high interest, in particular with respect to applications in regenerative medicine, renal disease modelling and nephrotoxicology (Little, 2016, Rak-Raszewska and Vainio, 2016, Schmidt-Ott, 2016, Schutgens et al. , 2016, Volovelsky and Kopan, 2016).
The group of Ryuichi Nishinakamura and colleagues at Kumamoto University (Japan) have developed two different protocols for the generation of PSC-based kidney organoids, which were based on either mESC or hiPSC (Fig. 4) (Taguchi et al. , 2014). These complex multi-step protocols were inspired by an extensive analysis of renal precursor cell populations in murine embryos. In case of the mESC-based protocol first embryoid bodies (EB) were generated, which were induced to form metanephric nephron progenitor (MNP)-like structures. Co-culture with mouse embryonic spinal cord or Wnt4-expressing cells led to formation of three-dimensional (3D) kidney organoids (Fig. 4). Such organoids contained renal tubule- and glomerulus-like structures that expressed multiple markers specific for PT, DT and podocytes.
## Figure 4: Protocols for the differentiation of PSC into kidney organoids
When mESC-derived induced EB were implanted beneath the kidney capsule of immunodeficient mice together with spinal cord, a similar formation of tubular and glomerulus-like structures was observed (Taguchi et al. , 2014). Excitingly, blood vessels were found in the implanted tissue, and the glomerulus-like structures became vascularized and contained red blood cells. The results suggested that the glomeruluslike structures became connected to the host circulation. This is an essential prerequisite for the formation of functional kidney tissue in a host after implantation. These are exciting results that suggest potential of this approach for applications in regenerative medicine, which will be discussed below (3.3).
Taguchi et al. (2014) developed also a variant of this protocol that is suitable for hiPSC-derived EB (Fig.4). MNP-like structures expressing WT1, PAX2, spalt like transcription factor (SALL)1 and SIX2 were obtained on day 14. Also in this case tubulogenesis and clustered podocyte formation could be induced by in vitro cocultivation with murine embryonic spinal cords. Immunostaining with antibodies against WT1 (podocytes), cadherin 6 (PT) and E-cadherin (DT) suggested that 3D kidney tissue-like structures were obtained that contained nephron-like arrangements of marker-expressing cells, where the different parts appeared to be connected in the same order as in kidney tissue. In case of this hiPSC-based approach results on additional markers or functional tests were not published.
The group of Melissa Little (The University of Queensland and The University of Melbourne Australia) and collaborators have established various protocols for the generation of interacting renal-like cellular compartments and kidney organoids. The first protocols were based on hESC and involved step-wise induction of PS-like and IM-like structures (Fig. 4) (Takasato et al. , 2014). With one protocol, a mix of MMand ureteric epithelium (UE)-like structures could be obtained on day 17. MM and UE are the two early renal precursor structures that interact to generate the kidney (Fig. 2). Further cultivation until day 22 resulted in generation of podocyte-, PT- and CDlike cells, as revealed by the marker expression patterns. Thus, multiple interacting cellular compartments that are required for renal development had been generated by this protocol.
A second hESC-based procedure based on induction with CHIR99021, followed by cultivation with FGF9 and heparin and subsequent growth factor withdrawal, resulted in the formation of MM- and UE-like structures on day 18 (Fig. 4) (Takasato et al. , 2014). In the dissociation/re-aggregation assay the cells derived with this protocol integrated into all major cellular compartments of the developing kidney. In order to test the impact of a 3D tissue environment, the cells were enzymatically dissociated on day 18 and pelleted by centrifugation. The 3D pellets were cultivated for another four days under conditions normally used for mouse embryonic kidney explant cultures. This resulted in the formation of self-organizing renal organoids with tubules that expressed either PT or UE markers, which were surrounded by WT1- and PAX2- positive MM-like structures (Takasato et al. , 2014). These structures were similar to those obtained with the dissociation/re-aggregation assay, confirming the capacity of the cells in the pellet to self-organize into renal tissue-like structures. This $2^{\mathrm{nd}}$ protocol established by Takasato et al., (2014) was then further developed into one of the current hiPSC-based methods for the generation of self-organizing kidney organoids (Takasato et al. , 2016, Takasato et al. , 2015).
This hiPSC-based method (Takasato et al. , 2016, Takasato et al. , 2015) also used induction of MM- and UE-like structures by CHIR99021 and FGF9/heparin in monolayer culture. The cells were then pelleted and the pellets were further treated as summarized in Figure 4, which resulted in the generation of self-organized kidney organoids. The kidney organoids contained nephron-like structures with different compartments (DT-, early loop of Henle-, PT- and glomerulus-like structures as revealed by marker expression patterns) that were connected with CD trees located at the bottom of the organoids, whereas the glomerular-like structures were located at the top. One organoid contained about 500 nephron-like structures (for comparison, a human kidney contains on average about one million nephrons). Endocytotic activity of PT-like structures was demonstrated with fluorescently labelled dextran. The glomerular-like structures were connected to such PT-like tubules and had a Bowman’s capsule and central podocyte-like cells with characteristic foot processes. The space surrounding the nephrons resembled the interstitium and contained cells expressing endothelial markers that formed a network with lumen formation, as well as cells expressing markers of perivascular cells, mesangial cells and stromal cells. Overall, these results showed that the organoids had a complex morphological patterning that resembled the organization of kidney tissue. Transcriptional profiling revealed highest congruence with first trimester human kidney (Takasato et al. , 2015).
The third group that contributed to the recent breakthrough in generating renal organoids was the group of Joseph V. Bonventre in Boston (USA). A multi-step protocol mimicking different developmental stages was developed for differentiating hESC or hiPSC into MNP-like structures (Morizane et al. , 2015) (Fig. 4). MNP-like cells expressed SIX2, SALL1, WT1 and PAX2 on day 12 after seeding (in the publication by Morizane et al. (2015) the differentiation timing was counted from the time point of CHIR99021 addition, which occurred 3 days after seeding and cultivation in the presence of FGF2; Fig. 4). MNP-like cells were further differentiated into renal vesicle (RV)-like structures, which expressed PAX8 and LHX1 and were organized into laminin-bound round clusters.
Further cultivation under 2D conditions after withdrawal of growth factors led to the formation of nephron-like structures, which expressed markers of podocytes (nephrin (NPHS1), podocalyxin like (PODXL)), PT (cadherin 2/N-cadherin (CHD2/NCAD) and loops of Henle/distal tubules (cadherin 1/E-cadherin (CDH1/ECAD), uromodulin (UMOD)) (Fig. 4) (Morizane et al. , 2015). Some structures could be also decorated with Lotus tetragonolobus lectin (LTL), which specifically binds to PT. 3D conditions were obtained by plating into ultra-low attachment plates on day 12 after initial cell seeding. Kidney organoids were obtained under 3D conditions from day 24 onwards (Fig. 4). They contained clusters of podocyte-like cells embraced by Bowman’s capsule-like structures, and tubular structures positive for PT markers (LTL, AQP1), markers for descending limbs of Henle (CDH1, AQP1), thick ascending limbs of Henle (CDH1, UMOD) and distal convoluted tubules (CDH1), which were arranged in a nephron-like order. Podocyte-like cells displayed structures resembling foot processes. Tubular structures had a lumen and epithelial tight junctions, and some tubular structures displayed a brush border, which is characteristic for PT (Morizane et al. , 2015).
CD markers or structures resembling UB derivatives were not observed by Morizane et al., (2015), and the organoids seemed to contain nephron-like structures, but no other renal structures or endothelial-like cells. In contrast, endothelial-like cells were contained in organoids derived with alternative protocols (Freedman et al. , 2015, Takasato et al. , 2015). Morizane et al. (2015) did also not report any evidence for interstitial fibroblast-like cells, which were observed in the organoids generated by Takasato et al., (2015), as well as CD-like structures.
An alternative protocol developed by the group of Bonventre and collaborators (Freedman et al. , 2015) is shorter and simpler in comparison to the method of Morizane et al., (2015) (Fig. 4). Organoids generated with this protocol (Freedman et al. , 2015) contained nephron-like structures and in addition, as mentioned, also endothelial-like cells that were arranged into cords and expressed the endothelial markers CD31 and von Willebrand factor. For generating renal organoids, hiPSC or hESC were sandwiched with Matrigel (Freedman et al. , 2015). Under these conditions cavitated spheroids were formed, that represented an epiblast-like stage. Epiblast-like structures were induced with CHIR99021 for 36 hours, followed by cultivation in B27-supplemented medium. The entire protocol comprised at least 16 days, and after day 10 after initial seeding organoids started to form that contained nephron-like tubular structures. Such structures reacted with LTL (PT marker) and coexpressed the PT-specific endocytotic receptors megalin and cubilin. Some apical microvilli and tight junctions were also displayed by the tubular structures. Lack of a fully differentiated phenotype was suggested by the fact that tubular structures expressed the nephron progenitor/RV markers LIM homeobox (LHX)1 and PAX2. Also the progenitor marker SIX2 was expressed in cells adjacent to the tubules. Expression of CDH1, LTL reactivity, and co-expression of podocyte markers (WT1 and synaptopodin) were observed in a nephron-like order (Freedman et al. , 2015).
The highest differentiation efficiency was obtained with hESC, with about 90 organoids per $1.9\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}$ , and the total culture contained about $25\%$ of LTL-reactive cells. The organoids had a variabile cellular composition, and about $80\%$ of LTLreactive organoids contained CD31- and PODXL-expressing cells. In addition, separate clusters of cells were observed, that expressed the neuroectodermal marker neuron-specific class III $\upbeta$ -tubulin. Such clusters occurred in a ratio of about 1:2 to kidney organoids (Freedman et al. , 2015).
Time course studies revealed sequential induction of mesoderm and nephron progenitor markers and PT and podocyte markers. When dissociated organoid cultures were implanted into kidneys of newborn immunodeficient mice, the human cells formed LTL epithelial structures in the kidney cortex.
In order to test for transport properties and barrier functions, tubular organoids were incubated with fluorescently labelled dextran ( $\mathrm{10\kDa)}$ and lucifer yellow. Lucifer yellow is a fluorescent organic anion that is normally transported into the PT lumen by drug transporters expressed in the PT. However, while labelled dextran was enriched in the tubular lumina after several hours of incubation, lucifer yellow was not (Freedman et al. , 2015). This suggests lack of expression of the apical efflux transporter multidrug resistance protein (MRP) 4 and/or basolateral organic anion transporters that handle renal clearance of lucifer yellow, and are also important for the renal handling of many other organic anions and drugs (Masereeuw et al. , 1999, Masereeuw et al. , 2003). Accumulation of labelled methotrexate in tubular lumina of organoids was variable (Freedman et al. , 2015), again pointing to problems with consistent expression of functional basolateral organic anion transporters and apical efflux transporters. Reproducible and consistent expression of functional transporters would be important for successful applications of renal organoids in various areas, including compound screening, in vitro toxicology and regenerative medicine. In addition to the PSC-based approaches discussed here, chimeric kidney organoids have been generated, which were derived from murine embryonic kidney cells mixed with human amniotic fluid stem cells (Xinaris et al. , 2016). After engraftment in vivo, vascularized glomeruli and tubular structures were obtained, and human cells differentiated into podocytes with slit diaphragms. This approach has potential for applications in translational research.
## 3. Applications
There are various potential applications of PSC-derived renal-like cells, precursor structures and organoids in bioartificial kidneys, disease models, regenerative medicine, compound efficacy screening and in vitro toxicology. Although potential applications in regenerative medicine are most attractive, the development of such approaches is in its infancy. The organization of kidney- organoids resembles to a certain degree the structure of kidney tissue. However, kidney organoids have not been shown to contain functional kidney tissue, and it is still a long way to go in order to realize applications of kidney organoids in regenerative medicine (Davies, 2015). Therefore, the development of applications in disease modelling and compound screening/in vitro toxicology is currently of greater practical importance, and PSC-based in vitro platforms for the accurate prediction of nephrotoxicity in humans are already available. The state of the art in this area will be discussed in the following section.
3.1.1. Established screening platforms for the efficient and accurate prediction of compound-induced PTC toxicity and injury mechanisms in humans
The kidney is one of the main target organs in the human body for the toxic effects of drugs, environmental toxicants, industrial chemicals, food additives and natural compounds (Guo and Nzerue, 2002, Jha and Rathi, 2008, Naughton, 2008, Tiong et al. , 2014, Van Vleet and Schnellmann, 2003). PTC are frequently affected for several reasons. On the one hand, compounds in the glomerular filtrate become highly concentrated in PT due to massive water reabsorption. In addition, PTC play prominent roles in compound transport, metabolism and clearance. Many drugs, xenobiotics and uremic toxins cannot be efficiently cleared by glomerular filtration, and such compounds are actively transported by PTC from the bloodstream into the glomerular filtrate. This is achieved by a large variety of transporters for drugs, xenobiotics and endogenous compounds, which are expressed at the baso-lateral and apical sides of PTC (Anzai et al. , 2005, Morrissey et al. , 2013, Morrissey et al. , 2012, Tiong et al. , 2014). Transport into the glomerular filtrate and also reabsorption from the glomerular filtrate leads to high intracellular accumulation of some compounds. An example is the antibiotic gentamicin, which is toxic for PTC and can induce acute kidney injury (AKI) already at therapeutic doses (Quiros et al. , 2011).
In addition to transporters, PTC express many enzymes for phase I and phase II metabolism, and the renal metabolism of compounds contributes to their clearance (Lash et al. , 2008, Lohr et al. , 1998). However, some compounds, such as the anti-cancer drug ifosfamide, are converted into toxic metabolites, which damage PTC (Ciarimboli et al. , 2011, Nissim et al. , 2006).
As PTC are frequently affected by compound-induced nephrotoxicity, this cell type is most attractive for the development of in vitro screening platforms. Cell-based in vitro platforms are of increasing importance in toxicology (Knudsen et al. , 2015, Westmoreland and Carmichael, 2014). In vitro platforms for the prediction of compound-induced PTC damage in humans have been recently established (Kandasamy et al. , 2015, Li et al. , 2014, Li et al. , 2013, Su et al. , 2014, Su et al. , 2015, Tiong et al. , 2014). The sensitivity and balanced accuracy of these platforms is in the range of about $70\%$ - $90\%$ , depending on the platform and cell type used.
The first of these platforms used compound-induced expression of interleukin 6 (IL6) and IL8 (CXCL8) as endpoint (Li et al. , 2013, Su et al. , 2014). Pre-validation was performed with $>40$ compounds that are known to be toxic or not toxic for PTC in humans. (The latter group contained compounds that are nephrotoxic, but not directly toxic for PTC in humans, as well as not nephrotoxic compounds. It should be noted that many of these compounds are also known to be toxic for other human organs and cell types, which is useful for testing the specificity of the method for predicting PTC toxicity). The predictivity of the platform was determined with different cell types, such as LLC-PK1 cells (widely used porcine PTC line), HK-2 cells (widely used human PTC line) and HPTC from different donors. Use of HPTC resulted in high predictivity.
In parallel, the first protocol based on human PSC for the generation of HPTC-like cells was established (Fig. 3) (Narayanan et al. , 2013). The methodologies were combined, which resulted in the first stem cell-based model for the prediction of nephrotoxicity in humans (Chuah et al. , 2016, Li et al. , 2014). Endpoint of this model was compound-induced expression of IL6 and CXCL8 (IL8), and the model had a balanced accuracy of $76\%$ , as determined by screening of $>.40$ compounds that are known to be toxic or not toxic for PTC in humans.
This model still had several disadvantages, such as the use of a hESC-based protocol (Narayanan et al. , 2013). The use of hESC is associated with various ethical and legal issues and, although the protocol was a simple 1-step protocol, the differentiation period was relatively long (Fig. 3). For these reasons an alternative hiPSC-based protocol was developed for the generation of HPTC-like cells. This resulted in the establishment of the shortest and most simple protocol available for the generation of a more differentiated renal cell type (Kandasamy et al. , 2015) (Fig. 3). Also the protocol by Kandasamy et al. (2015) comprised only one single step, which was supplementation of the cell culture medium with BMP2 and BMP7 on day 1 after seeding (Fig. 3). HPTC-like cells with a purity of $>90\%$ were ready for compound screening on day 8 after only one week of differentiation (Kandasamy et al. , 2015) (Figs. 3 and 5). As no harvesting before screening was required due to the high purity of the cells, the cells could be differentiated in multi-well plates, and the same multi-well plates could be used subsequently for compound screening on day 8. The currently used screening protocol used 16 hours of compound exposure, and the screening results could be determined on day 9 by using either the IL6/CXCL8-based method (expression levels determined by qPCR) or high-content screening (HCS; Figs. 5 and 6) (Kandasamy et al. , 2015).
This hiPSC-based model for the prediction of PTC toxicity in humans has been pre-validated with 30 compounds that are toxic or not toxic for PTC in humans (Kandasamy et al. , 2015). The results were automatically classified by machine learning methods and the predictive performance of the model was determined by using a 10-fold cross-validation procedure. The training sensitivity (sensitivity is defined as the percentage of compounds that are toxic for PTC in humans and give a positive test result in vitro), specificity (specificity is defined as percentage of compounds that are not toxic for PTC in humans and give a negative in vitro test result) and balanced accuracy (mean of sensitivity and specificity) were $100\%$ , showing that all PTC-toxic and non-PTC-toxic compounds could be correctly separated. The test sensitivity, specificity and balanced accuracy were $89\%,85\%$ and $87\%$ , respectively, which means that $87\%$ of the predictions made with this platform with respect to the human PTC toxicity of a compound would be correct (Fig. 6) (Kandasamy et al. , 2015).
An interesting question is whether this predictive method could be also used with extended compound exposure periods, and whether it would be suitable for chronic and repeated dose toxicity. Static in vitro models typically use exposure periods between 12 – 72 hours. hiPSCderived HPTC-like cells are stable until at least day 11 after starting differentiation (day 10 results are shown in Kandasamy et al., 2015). As compound exposure starts on day 8, this model would suitable for 72 hours of exposure. However, such extended exposure times were not required, so far, as this hiPSC-based model specifically detected also after 16 hours of exposure compounds contained within the test set that are associated with chronic and repeated dose PT toxicity in humans. Examples are rifampicin and tacrolimus, which are inducers of interleukin 6 (IL6) and CXCL8 in the iPSC-based in vitro model .
It is important to note that these endpoints measured in the in vitro model (IL6 and CXCL8 expression) are different from endpoints measured in vivo. While toxicity is typically detected in humans in vivo only when clinical signs of toxicity become apparent, the in vitro model measures subtle sub-lethal cellular changes that occur early after compound exposure and that may not be associated with any clinical signs of toxicity when occurring in vivo, but can be used to predict the PT toxicity of a compound.
Exposure periods longer than 72 hours often require sophisticated models, such as microfluidic models. Such models are currently too slow for testing larger numbers of compounds and are therefore of unknown predictivity (Jang et al. , 2013).
HCS was used to address whether the platform based on HPTC-like cells did not only have a high predictivity, but could also correctly identify compound-induced mechanisms and cellular pathways (Kandasamy et al. , 2015). . This work was performed with cisplatin and aristolochic acid, which are toxic for PTC in humans. In addition, acarbose and ethylene glycol were included, which are not directly toxic for PTC. By using the hiPSC-based platform it was found that aristolochic acid and cisplatin induced generation of reactive oxygen species and a DNA damage response and triggered a main inflammatory pathway (Kandasamy et al. , 2015). This was in agreement with human clinical data and the known cellular mechanisms and pathways triggered by these compounds. In contrast, acarbose and ethylene glycol remained negative in all assays (Kandasamy et al. , 2015). Of note, although these compounds are not directly toxic for PTC, but they are toxic for other organ systems in humans, such as the liver and the peripheral and central nervous system. The fact that these compounds gave negative results with the hiPSC-based renal platform underlined the high specificity of this platform with respect to PTC injury. In addition, the results, which were obtained by HCS, demonstrated the compatibility of this platform with HCS and its ability to correctly identify compound-induced mechanisms and cellular pathways .
Figure 6: Platforms for the accurate prediction of compound-induced PTC toxicity in humans.
Due to the requirement for testing high numbers of chemicals and potential toxicants, highthroughput assays are of increasing importance in in vitro toxicology (Judson et al. , 2013). Therefore, a predictive PTC-based screening platform was developed that was solely based on HCS (Fig. 6) (Su et al. , 2015). The platform was pre-validated with $>40$ compounds by using HK-2 cells and HPTC. Compound-treated cells were screened by high-content imaging. Image analysis was performed by phenotypic profiling with the cellXpress platform (Laksameethanasan et al. , 2013), and 129 phenotypic cellular features and their changes in response to compound treatment were assessed. Sets of 4-5 cellular features that were highly predictive for PTC toxicity in humans were identified by machine learning. Machine learning was also used for determining the predictive performance, and the values for test sensitivity and test specificity ranged between ${\sim}80\%\ {\sim}\ 5\ 95\%$ . The test balanced accuracy was $82\%$ (HPTC) and $89\%$ (HK-2 cells), respectively (Fig. 6) (Su et al. , 2015).
Apart from applications in compound safety screening, also efficacy screening in drug discovery and development would be a promising application (Fig. 5). For instance, PTC reabsorb most of the glucose from the glomerular filtrate and transport it back into the bloodstream. Essential for glucose reabsorption are the sodium/glucose cotransporters SGLT1 and SGLT2, which are located at the apical PTC membrane facing the glomerular filtrate. The low affinity/high capacity transporter SGLT2 is inhibited by the latest generation of anti-diabetes drugs, which are already widely used and have an enormous market potential. Three drugs, namely canagliflozin, dapagliflozin and empagliflozin, have been approved to date (Moses et al. , 2014, Seufert, 2015, Wu et al. , 2016).
It has been shown that hiPSC-derived HPTC-like cells express SGLT1 and SGLT2 and also the baso-lateral glucose transporters GLUT1 and GLUT5 (Kandasamy et al. , 2015). In addition, these cells express various PTC-specific transporters and enzymes that are important for renal drug handling (Kandasamy et al. , 2015). These results suggest that hiPSC-derived HPTC-like cells have high potential for applications also in efficacy screening. Transporter and enzymatic functions of other PSC-derived renal-like cells and organoids (Figs. 3 and 4) have not been characterized in detail, so far.
Also the suitability of kidney organoids for assessing PTC toxicity has been addressed. In respective studies only 1 – 2 compounds with known PTC toxicity in humans (cisplatin or cisplatin and gentamicin) were applied to kidney organoids, and no non-PTC-toxic compounds were included (Freedman et al. , 2015, Morizane et al. , 2015, Takasato et al. , 2015).
For instance, Takasato et al. (2015) treated kidney organoids with 0, 5, 20 and $100~{\upmu\mathbf{M}}$ of cisplatin for 24 hours. Cisplatin injures PTC and distal tubular cells (DTC) in humans. Both 5 $\upmu\mathrm{m}$ and $20~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ of cisplatin (results for $100~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ were not shown) induced apoptosis in kidney organoids in LTL+ CHD1/ECAD+ cells. Such cells were called “mature PT”. No significant apoptosis was induced by cisplatin in organoids in LTL CDH1/ECAD cells, which were called “immature PT”.
The specificity and predictivity of such kidney organoid-based approaches is unknown due to the low numbers of compounds tested and the lack of testing non-toxic compounds and controls. Testing of larger numbers of nephrotoxic and not nephrotoxic compounds would be required in order to determine whether renal organoid-based approaches would be useful.
Achieving a higher throughput with these models for testing larger numbers of compounds would be difficult for several reasons. On the one hand, the differentiation protocols are complex and long differentiation periods are required (Fig. 4). This makes the methods not only slow, but also cost prohibitive. On the other hand, kidney organoids represent complex 3D structures, which are not compatible with current HCS technologies. Also, obtaining reproducible quantitative readouts, as required in compound screening, would be difficult.
This would be due to the fact that the numbers of organoids per well were variable, and not all 3D structures in a well represented renal organoids (Freedman et al. , 2015). Also the sizes and cellular compositions of renal organoids were variable and complex, leading to variable cell numbers and percentages of different cell types per well. For instance, only about $80\%$ of LTL+ organoids contained also CD31- and PODXL-expressing cells (Freedman et al. , 2015). It is currently also still unclear whether cells contained in renal organoids displayed a fully mature differentiated phenotype, which could be a potential advantage of organoid-based approaches At least in some cases it was observed that nephron-like tubules in organoids expressed nephron progenitor and RV markers, which suggested that the tubular cells did not express a fully differentiated phenotype.
## 3.2. Potential applications of hiPSC-derived HPTC-like cells in bioartificial kidneys
End stage renal disease (ESRD) patients that receive dialysis have high rates of morbidity and mortality and a dramatically compromised quality of life, unless they receive a kidney transplant (Bayat et al. , 2010, Oniscu et al. , 2005, Tasnim et al. , 2010, Wolfe et al. , 1999). Also, the rates of mortality of critically ill patients with AKI remain high and unchanged since several decades, ranging between $40\%-70\%$ (Tolwani, 2012, Uchino et al. , 2005). Partial removal of uremic toxins and volume control, as achieved by hemodialysis or hemofiltration, does not fully replace renal functions. The kidney has various important functions in addition to removal of uremic toxins, including endocrinologic functions, production of the most active form of vitamin D, acid-base balance control, reabsorption of glucose, peptides, electrolytes and other compounds that are still useful for the human body, active transport and metabolism of xenobiotics, and probably also immunomodulatory functions (Brenner, 2008, Curthoys and Moe, 2014, Fraser and Kodicek, 1970, Tasnim et al. ,
2010). PTC play important roles in many of these functions, and are therefore included in the bioreactor unit of bioartificial kidneys in order to replace renal functions more comprehensively (Humes et al. , 1999, Jansen et al. , 2014, Saito et al. , 2011, Tasnim et al. , 2010).
Figure 7: Bioartificial kidneys with hiPSC-derived HPTC-like cells.
A bioartificial kidney consists of a hemofilter in series with a bioreactor unit, which contains PTC (Fig. 7) (Humes et al. , 1999, Jansen et al. , 2014, Oo et al. , 2013, Saito et al. , 2011, Tasnim et al. , 2010). The hemofilter is used for normal hemofiltration in order to replace glomerular functions. The hemofiltrate and blood leaving the hemofilter are then directed into different compartments of the bioreactor unit, which are separated by porous membranes. PTC are residing on one side of the porous membrane, which is typically the side that is exposed to the hemofiltrate (and not to the blood). This would mimick the situation in the renal tubule, where PTC are exposed to the glomerular filtrate. PTC residing on the porous semi-permeable membrane could then control the exchange of compounds between the hemofiltrate and the blood, similar as in the renal tubule in the kidney. This would include reabsorption of glucose, proteins and other compounds from the hemofiltrate into the blood, as well as active transport of uremic toxins and xenobiotics into the hemofiltrate. In addition, the cells could perform other PTC functions, such as acid-base control or production of 1,25- dihydroxy-vitamin D3.
Typically, commercial hemodialysis/hemofiltration cartridges are used for both units of a bioartificial kidney. Such cartridges contain semi-permeable hollow fiber membranes (HFM).
As mentioned, PTC are seeded in the bioreactor unit on the side of the HFM which is exposed to the hemofiltrate, while the blood flows on the other side of the HFM.
Bioartificial kidneys have been applied in animal studies (Fissell et al. , 2002, Fissell et al. , 2003, Humes et al. , 1999, Humes et al. , 2002, Saito et al. , 2012) and in clinical trials (Humes et al. , 2004, Tumlin et al. , 2008) to critically ill patients with AKI or respective animal models. Improved survival has been observed in animal studies, but the results were inconsistent with respect to clinical trials, and a better understanding of the effects of bioartificial kidneys would be required.
For clinical applications only HPTC have been approved. The source for these primary human renal cells is limited, and huge cell numbers are required for a bioreactor that would be suitable for clinical applications. A bioreactor suitable for the treatment of one patient contains more than ${10}^{9}$ cells (Humes et al. , 2002).  Therefore, the cell sourcing problem is one of the most serious problems associated with bioartificial kidneys. Also, primary cells are affected by interdonor variability, which is another problem with respect to clinical applications. The use of PTC lines immortalized with genes affecting cell growth and survival is unlikely to be approved for clinical applications for safety reasons.
hiPSC-derived PTC-like or HPTC-like cells provide an interesting alternative to human primary renal cells. Protocols for generating such cell types are available (Fig. 3). hiPSC can be propagated indefinitely and HPTC-like cells generated from the same hiPSC line would not display interdonor variability. For safety reasons it would be important to select hiPSC lines that have been reprogrammed with episomal constructs. Loss of these constructs during passaging can be monitored and confirmed by sensitive PCR techniques. Potential teratogenicity of residual undifferentiated hiPSC in clinical applications would be a less serious issue with respect to bioartificial kidneys (in comparison to regenerative cell therapies), as the cells would be separated from the bloodstream by semipermeable membranes, and therefore could not enter the patient’s body.
In summary, hiPSC-based approaches could potentially solve one of the biggest problems associated with bioartificial kidneys, which is identification of a reliable source for large numbers of PTC, that have a reproducible high quality and are not affected by safety issues associated with the use of viral oncogenes. It would be exciting to see results obtained with bioartificial kidneys containing hiPSC-derived HPTC- or PTC-like cells in the future. Essential for such approaches would be the controlled mass production of hiPSC in advanced bioreactor systems (Kropp et al. , 2016), which also allow controlled mass differentiation into specific lineages. This has been demonstrated with respect to hiPSC-derived cardiomyocytelike cells (Kempf et al. , 2016, Kempf et al. , 2014), but it would be expected that advanced bioreactor systems would be also suitable for the mass production of renal- and HPTC-like cells.
## 3.3. Regenerative medicine
The numbers of ESRD patients are dramatically increasing in all industrialized countries due to lifestyle changes and ageing populations (Lin, 2003, Rutkowski, 2000, Vathsala, 2007). There are two options for ESRD patients: dialysis or a renal transplant. As mentioned above, dialysis is life-saving, but is associated with high rates of morbidity and mortality and a highly reduced quality of life. Although a kidney transplant can completely replace renal functions and usually tremendously improves the quality of life of ESRD patients, sufficient numbers of transplants are not available. For instance, in 2013 over 120,000 ESRD patients were awaiting donation in the United States only (Matas et al. , 2013). On this background technologies for regenerating diseased kidneys with stem cell-derived renal cells or for growing transplantable organs would be very much welcomed.
Renal organoids are attractive with respect to regenerative therapies, and they contain various renal-like cell types arranged into structures resembling the micro-structure of a kidney. However, these structures have not been shown to be functional, and organoids also do not recapitulate the overall tissue organization of an entire kidney (Davies, 2015). For instance, there is no connection to a urine drain, and also the urine-concentrating structures that are normally found in the renal medulla are missing. Also connection to the host vasculature would be cirticial for obtaining functional kidney tissue.
Figure 8: Potential applications in regenerative medicine.
Although it would still be a long way to go until transplantable kidneys can be grown from renal organoids, encouraging results have been obtained by Taguchi et al. (2014) by using an approach based on implantation of renal organoid progenitors (Fig. 8). This group implanted mESC-derived MNP-like structures together with spinal cords beneath the kidney capsule of immunodeficient mice (Fig. 4). Massive tubulogenesis and formation of glomerular-like structures had occurred after one week in the implants. Excitingly, the implants had become vascularized by the host. This involved also the glomerular-like structures, which contained red blood cells. Vascularization seemed to be due to the expression of vascular growth factors by the graft tissue, especially by the glomerular podocyte-like cells (Taguchi et al. , 2014).
This work was continued in a follow up study by Sharmin et al. (2016), which was performed by using the hiPSC-based protocol established by Taguchi et al. (2014). However, Sharmin et al. (2016) generated hiPSC where the green fluorescent protein (GFP) gene was integrated into the nephrin (NPHS1) locus. NPHS1 is expressed in podocytes and GFP expression allowed to visualize formation of podocyte-like cells in vitro, which displayed apicobasal polarity with nephrin accumulated at the basal side. These cells also formed primary processes and slit-diaphragm-like structures, and generated glomerular-like structures in vitro. Remarkably, self-organization into glomerular-like structures occurred in the absence of other glomerular cell types (mesangial and endothelial cells). Sorted podocyte-like cells displayed transcription profiles that overlapped with those of determined for human and murine podocyes in vivo (Sharmin et al. , 2016).
Excitingly, after transplantation of hiPSC-derived nephron progenitors beneath murine kidney capsules with a new tension-releasing technique, formation of vascularized glomerular-like structures occured. This was the first demonstration of the formation of such structures derived from hiPSC. Vascularized glomerular-like structures displayed slit diaphragm-like structures, a basement membrane, and fenestrated endothelial cells. Intriguingly, the endothelial cells in hiPSC-derived glomerular-like structures were of mouse origin, and co-transplanted HUVEC did not appear to be competent (Sharmin et al. , 2016). In the future it would be important to identify a suitable source of human endothelial cells, and to achieve further maturation of glomerular-like structures in vitro and in vivo, as well as including additional glomerular cells types.
Renal organoids that already contain endothelial-like cells may be most attractive for implantation, and such organoids have been generated (Freedman et al. , 2015, Takasato et al. , 2015) (Fig. 4). Alternatively vascularization can be potentially enhanced by treatment of organoids with vascular endothelial growth factor (VEGF) and other growth factors promoting vascularization (Hariharan et al. , 2015, Xinaris et al. , 2012), and VEGF was also used in most implants in the study by Sharmin et al. (2016), although vascularization occurred also in the absence of added VEGF.
An alternative to implanting in vitro-generated renal-like (progenitor) tissue may be injection of PSC-derived renal-like (progenitor) cells into diseased kidneys (Fig. 8). This has been done recently with hiPSC-derived OSR+ SIX2+ renal-like progenitor cells, which were injected into the kidney capsule of a murine AKI model (Toyohara et al. , 2015). This suppressed functional and histological signs of kidney damage, and these beneficial effects were due to renoprotective factors secreted by the renal-like progenitor cells.
Imberti et al. (2015) infused hiPSC-derived renal progenitor cells intravenously into a murine model of cisplatin-induced AKI. Renal progenitor cells were obtained with a 2-step protocol employing small molecule inhibitors, RA, AA, FGF2, BMP7 and glial-derived neurotrophic factor. Intravenously infused renal progenitor cells showed tropism for the kidney and integrated preferentially into damaged proximal tubuli in the murine AKI model. This was associated with a substantial reduction of tubular damage, increased proliferation of tubular cells and improvement of renal injury (Imberti et al. , 2015). Currently the mechanisms are unclear and it needs to be addressed whether these improvements were due to incorporation of hiPSC-dereived cells into damaged tubuli or due to activation of the proliferation of remaining tubular cells or both. However, these results are highly encouraging and intravenous administration of hiPSC-derived cells would be compatible with clinical applications.
Another promising approach would be to generate implants by re-populating de-cellularized kidney extracellular matrix (ECM) scaffolds with PSC-derived cells (Fig. 8). Recently, substantial progress has been made in re-populating de-cellularized kidney scaffolds (McKee and Wingert, 2016). The most inspiring study in this respect has been performed by Song et al., (2013), and scaffolds from rat, porcine and human kidneys were used (Song et al. , 2013). These scaffolds were not yet re-populated with PSC-derived cells (PSC-based approaches were not available at that time), but rat neonatal kidney cells (NKC; injected via the ureter) were used together with human umbilical vein endothelial cells (HUVEC; injected via the renal artery). While HUVEC were found to re-populate the vascular compartment, NKC formed tubular compartments and expressed some tubular markers. Glomeruli engrafted with podocytes that formed foot processes were also observed. After implantation into rats the bioartifical scaffolds produced some liquid that resembled urine, but it seemed that cellular functions were immature (Song et al. , 2013).
A similar approach with hiPSC-derived renal progenitor-like and endothelial-like cells has been published recently (Du et al. , 2016). In this case de-cellularized murine kidney scaffolds were re-seeded with the different cell types, and renal progenitor-like cells had been obtained by step-wise differentiation of hiPSC with AA, BMP4, LiCl and RA (Nishikawa et al. , 2012). After implantation into mice glomerular-like structures containing endothelial-like and renal progenitor-like cells of human origin were formed, and endothelial-like cells seemed to play a critical role in this process (Du et al. , 2016).
In the future, kidney scaffolds for clinical applications could be obtained from porcine or discarded human kidneys (Guan et al. , 2015, Orlando et al. , 2013, Orlando et al. , 2012, Peloso et al. , 2015). In order to re-populate acellular scaffolds with a broader variety of different renal-like cell types, use of dissociated renal organoids may be useful (Fig. 8). Critical would be the maturation of hiPSC-derived cells into fully functional renal-like cell types. In addition, the use of perfusion bioreactor systems for the re-population of renal scaffolds would be important (Uzarski et al. , 2014).
hiPSC-based approaches would have the advantage that immunocompatible tissue could be bioengineered if autologous cells from the same patient would be used (Wilm et al. , 2016). Also, phenotyping of the large and growing number of hiPSC lines in combination with establishing respective databases and cell banks would help to find hiPSC lines that would match a particular patient that requires a bioengineered organ. This could help to reduce the use of immunsuppressive drugs, which have chronic nephrotoxicity in transplant patients (Benigni et al. , 1999, Tiong et al. , 2014).
The generation of autologous cells could be greatly facilitated by direct reprogramming of patient-derived cells into induced renal cells, which would bypass the time-consuming steps of hiPSC generation and differentiation. Intriguingly, a protocol based on combined expression of specific transcription factors has been developed recently for the reprogramming of mouse and human fibroblasts into induced renal tubular epithelial cells (Kaminski et al. , 2016). Such cells integrated into renal organoids and formed tubules in decellularized renal scaffolds. It would be exciting to see applications of this technology in regenerative medicine.
## 3.4. Bioprinted renal tissue
An alternative to using de-cellularized and re-seeded kidney scaffolds, as discussed above, may be bioprinting of kidney tissue (Fig. 8). Due to the complex micro-architecture of the kidney, which involves many different cell types arranged into delicate structures, bioprinting of this organ is particularly challenging. Anthony Atala’s group at the Wake Forest Institute for Regenerative Medicine (Winston-Salem, NC, USA) is working on bioprinting approaches with the ultimate goal to produce a transplantable organ https://www.ted.com/talks/anthony_atala_printing_a_human_kidney?language=en
However, functional kidney tissue or whole organs for transplantation have not been obtained by bioprinting, so far (Ledford, 2015). Organovo (San Diego, CA, USA) generates kidney tissue by bioprinting for compound screening and disease modelling http://3dprintingindustry.com/2015/04/01/organovo-announces-its-first-3d-bioprintedkidney-tissue/ . Also these layered constructs do not represent functional renal tissue. So far, only few well-known nephrotoxic drugs have been tested with the bioprinted constructs in order to establish a model for in vitro nephrotoxicology. The predictivity of the bioprinted model is currently unknown due to the small numbers of compounds tested. Organovo has licensed the renal organoid technology developed in Melissa Little’s group (Takasato et al. , 2014, Takasato et al. , 2015) in order to obtain a source of PSC-derived kidney cells for bioprinting http://3dprintingindustry.com/2015/10/13/organovo-licenses-mini-kidneys-forbioprinting/.
Recently, 3D convoluted channels have been bioprinted on microfluidic chips, and these channels were seeded with renal proximal tubular cells (Homan et al. , 2016). No PSCderived cells were used in this approach, which employed a human proximal tubular cell line that was immortalized with human telomerase reverse transcriptase (PTEC-TERT1). However, this approach may be compatible with PSC-derived cells.
By using a removable substrate, first a convoluted structure was printed on the chip, which became embedded in a gelatin-fibrin hydrogel. Subsequently, the convoluted structure was removed, leaving a channel within the hydrogel, which was then seeded with PTEC-TERT1 cells. The cell-seeded channels were then perfused, and channels with diameters between \~ $400-550~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ were used for further experiments (Homan et al. , 2016). These diameters are still much larger than the diameters of proximal tubules in the human kidney, which have diameters in the range of $\sim65~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ . As mentioned above, bioprinting of the delicate renal structures is still a major technical problem. The most interesting potential application of such bioprinted channels containing proximal tubular cells would be in compound screening. So far, also with these bioprinted proximal tubular cell-seeded channels, only one nephrotoxic compound has been tested (no non-nephrotoxic control compounds were included), and the predictivity of this approach is unknown. Also, formation of confluent epithelia in the channels required a maturation period of 3 weeks (Homan et al. , 2016), which limits the efficiency of this approach.
Cell performance and maturation could potentially be improved by using improved bioengineered extracellular matrices. In this respect, an interesting method has been developed by Nagao et al. (2016). This group has generated hydrogels from de-cellularized human kidney cortex, which was achieved by pepsin digestion of human de-cellularized kidney cortex in HCl and subsequent neutralization (Nagao et al. , 2016). Main applications of such hydrogels, which were mixed collagen I in order to increase the mechanical integrity, would be in bioengineered microphysiological systems in order to achieve improved cell performance. The approach developed by Nagao et al. (2016) has focused on improving the performance of endothelial cells with the goal to generate vascularized kidney chips, which are not available yet. Also, kidney-derived hydrogels have not been used for bioprinting yet. It would be exciting to combine now the different technologies discussed above, in order to generate bioprinted kidney chips with improved ECM and cell performance and a more complex tissue structure comprising vasculature, renal tubules, and probably other nephronand kidney-specific structures.
## 3.5. Kidney disease modelling
Renal disease modelling would be another important area for applications of PSC-derived renal cells. For many different types of chronic kidney diseases, there are no specific biomarkers and treatments available. Disease models could be helpful to improve understanding of the underlying pathophysiology, which would be required for developing biomarkers and specific treatment options. It would be critical to recapitulate the disease phenotype in vitro, and to link the phenotype to the disease-causative genotype. In this respect it would be favourable to work with hiPSC derived from families with hereditary kidney disease, or hiPSC derived from patients where the underlying disease-causative mutation is known already. Working on kidney diseases where the affected genes have been already identified would also be helpful, and such renal diseases can be also addressed with disease models based on genetically engineered PSC (Fig. 9).
A well-characterized genetic disorder that is associated with progressive chronic kidney disease leading to ESRD is polycystic kidney disease (PKD). In PKD renal tubular structures are replaced by fluid-filled cysts, leading to massive enlargement and functional impairment of the kidneys. Cyst formation occurs already during fetal development in case of autosomal recessive PKD (ARPKD) (Nakanishi et al. , 2000), which is less common with an estimated prevalence of 1:20,000 live births. All typical cases of ARPKD are linked to mutations in the PKHD1 locus. The respective protein, fibrocystin, is localized in primary cilia and the basal bodies of renal cells (Sun et al. , 2011).
Autosomal dominant PKD (ADPKD) is more common with an estimated prevalence of 1:400 to 1:1000 live births. Typically, ESRD develops only later in life in ADPKD patients. ADPKD is linked to mutations in PKD1 or PKD2, which code for components of primary cilia of renal cells, and are probably involved in cell signalling (Freedman, 2015, Sun et al. , 2011).
Although various compounds for the potential treatment of ADPKD are examined (Sun et al. , 2011), specific treatment options are currently very limited. Tolvaptan, a vasopressin-2 receptor antagonist that had been already used for hyponatremia (Baur and Meaney, 2014), has been recently approved in Europe and other areas for the treatment of ADPKD, but FDA approval for treatment of ADPKD has not been obtained in the United States http://www.pkdcure.org/news/fda-responds-to-tolvaptan-new-drug-application . This drug slows down disease progression by interfering with fluid reabsorption into the cysts. Another potential treatment option is a triptolide-containing formulation, and a clinical trial is ongoing https://clinicaltrials.gov/ct2/show/NCT02115659 .
A hESC-based ADPKD renal disease model has been recently established by introducing biallelic truncating mutations into PKD1 or PKD2 (Freedman et al. , 2015). Cyst-like structures developed when the mutated hESC were applied in a renal organoid model. Such cysts were not observed when isogenic control hESC were used. The cysts developed from PTC-like cells in the renal organoids, as revealed by their affinity to LTL. Cysts could be observed at about 35 days after initial plating, and about $6\%$ of kidney organoids were associated with cysts (Freedman et al. , 2015) . Although this model could be potentially helpful in phenotypic screens for the discovery of potential drugs for ADPKD treatment, the efficiency would have to be improved. Given the low percentage of cyst formation, it is also unlikely that hiPSC from ADPKD patients can be used for disease modelling under these conditions, as their mutations are typically less severe than biallelic truncations (Freedman, 2015).
The study by Freedman et al., 2015 has also addressed the effects of biallelic truncation of PODXL. PODXL is a candidate gene for focal segmental glomerulosclerosis, which is associated with proteinuria and renal insufficiency (Barua et al. , 2014). PODXL is expressed by podocytes in normal human kidneys and by various other normal somatic cell types, and is also highly expressed in stem cells and some types of aggressive cancers (Nielsen and McNagny, 2009). Podocyte-like cells in renal organids derived from hESC with biallelic PODXL truncations showed an aberrant organization of cell juntions. These findings are in agreement with a proposed role of PODXL as regulator of podocyte junctions (Doyonnas et al. , 2001).
Overall, these first proof-of-principle studies suggest that PSC-based disease models could help to better understand the pathophysiology of kidney diseases. Such models may be useful for the development of screening platforms for drug discovery. For successful future applications it would be important to develop robust renal disease models, where disease phenotypes can be generated with high efficiency and reproducibility.
## 4. Conclusions
Whereas protocols for the directed differentiation of PSC into tissue-specific cell types had been well established with respect to various organs and tissues, such as liver, heart or cartilage, nothing comparable was available for the kidney. In 2013 a race has started on methods for the generation of PSC-derived renal-like cell types, precursors and kidney organoids, and more than a dozen protocols have been established. The first wave of breakthroughs in this area was related to the generation of renal-like cell types and precursors (Fig. 3), whereas the focus has shifted recently towards kidney organoids (Fig. 4).
Currently applications in in vitro compound screening are most attractive and most advanced.
A hiPSC-based in vitro screening platform for the prediction of compound-induced renal PTC injury in humans with a test balanced accuracy of $87\%$ has been established (Kandasamy et al. , 2015). PTC are a frequent target of compound-induced toxicity due to their roles in compound transport and metabolism (Tiong et al. , 2014). Essential for the development of this platform were also recent breakthroughs in the establishment of in vitro methods for the prediction of PTC toxicity in humans (Kandasamy et al. , 2015, Li et al. , 2014, Li et al. , 2013, Su et al. , 2014, Su et al. , 2015). The hiPSC-based method (Kandasamy et al. , 2015) is now combined with a powerful predictive renal high-throughput platform (Su et al. , 2015), which is currently used in collaboration with the US EPA in order to predict the human PTC toxicity of large numbers of industrial chemicals and environmental toxicants.
Predictive screening technologies based on kidney organoids have not been established, and kidney organoids may be more suitable for applications in regenerative medicine. Critical would be the generation of functional renal tissue. The functionality of nephron structures and cell types contained within kidney organoids has not been demonstrated yet, and organoids do also not contain various renal structures that are essential in a functional kidney (Davies, 2015). Critical would be also the connection to the host vasculature in case of renallike tissue implantation. Encouraging results have been obtained by Taguchi et al. (2014) and Sharmin et al. (2016) after implantation of PSC-derived renal precursors into the kidney of immunodeficient mice, where formation of glomerular-like structures was observed, which were vascularized  by the host (Sharmin et al. , 2016, Taguchi et al. , 2014).
PSC-derived renal-like cells and organoids are expected to play important roles in renal disease modelling in the future, and a first proof-of-concept study has been performed, demonstrating the successful recapitulation of renal disease phenotypes (Freedman et al. , 2015). Important in this area are the recent advancements in hiPSC-based technologies for the generation of patient-specific cell lines, and the great progress in genome editing for the introduction of specific mutations linked to renal disease (Freedman, 2015). In the future it would be essential to develop shorter and more efficient protocols for the generation of renal disease-specific phenotypes in vitro.
Exciting new possibilities are also emerging with the recent advancements in bioprinting (Hariharan et al. , 2015). Bioprinting with PSC-derived renal-like cells and kidney organoids is attractive with respect to applications in regenerative medicine and compound screening. Current approaches are in their infancy, and bioprinting of renal renal tissue is particularly challenging due to its complex microstructure. Breakthroughs in this area would largely depend on improving the resolution of bioprinting.
PSC-based approaches may also help to generate large quantities of human renal-like cells with a reproducible high quality, as required for bioartificial kidneys. Bioartificial kidneys have been shown to improve survival when applied to animal models of AKI (Fissell et al. , 2002, Fissell et al. , 2003, Humes et al. , 1999, Humes et al. , 2002, Saito et al. , 2012), but the results of clinical trials were inconsistent (Humes et al. , 2004, Tumlin et al. , 2008). A better understanding of the effects of bioartificial kidneys would be required in order to make further progress in this area.
In summary, various breakthroughs have been made recently with respect to generating PSCderived renal-like cells, precursors and kidney organoids. This opens exciting new possibilities for applications of renal-like cells and tissues, and first successful applications have been established in the area of in vitro safety screening. Important for the success in establishing novel applications in other areas would be the improvement of current protocols, and the capability to generate functional renal-like cells and tissues with high efficiency and reproducibility.
