Figure 1: Human kidney and nephron structure. The schematic drawing shows a crosssection of a human kidney (left). The enlargement (right) displays a nephron with a CD. The different parts of the kidney and nephron are indicated. At the bottom, the main areas of applications for stem cell-derived renal-like cells, precursors or organoids are indicated.
Figure 2: Embryonic kidney development and widely used markers. Mammalian embryos are schematically drawn at the top. Early embryonic renal precursor structures (PS and IM) are highlighted in red and green. The boxed area in the middle illustrates the invasion of the ureteric UB (violet) into the MM (pink) and branching morphogenesis. The MM at the tips of the UB branches gives rise to the MNP (light blue), from which the nephrons (bottom) are derived. The different parts of a nephron and the CD (the CD is a UBderived structure) are indicated and highlighted with different colors. The glomerulus contains glomerular endothelial cells and POD. The renal tubule consists of the PT, the loop of Henle and the DT. Characteristic markers that are expressed in the different structures and that were widely used in current studies on stem cell-derived renal-like cells and organoids are indicated. The acronyms follow the Human Genome Organisation (HUGO) nomenclature. In some cases alternative widely used acronyms are indicated after a slash. The same color code as used here was applied in Figures 3 and 4 to highlight different stages and renal-like cell types or structures.
Figure 4: Protocols for the differentiation of PSC into kidney organoids. Layout and structure are similar to Figure 3 (see legend of Fig. 3 for details). Specific culture conditions, like pellet or matrigel sandwich cultures and two-dimensional (2D) or 3D cultures, are indicated. Taguchi et al. (2014) used either in vitro conditions during the entire protocol, or applied after initial in vitro steps further differentiation under in vivo conditions after implantation, which is indicated by a waved line. Cloud-like structures on the right represent structures with multiple renal-like cell types or kidney organoids. Kidney organoids that contained vascular structures or endothelial-like cells are framed in red. Ep: epiblast, LPS: late primitive streak, NM: nascent mesoderm, PNM: posterior nascent mesoderm, PIM: posterior intermediate mesoderm , UE: ureteric epithelium, PA: pre-tubular aggregate, RV: renal vesicle, MEF CM: murine embryonic fibroblast-conditioned medium.
Figure 5: Flow diagram illustrating screening platforms based on hiPSC-derived HPTC-like cells. hiPSC are seeded on day 0 (top) into multi-well plates. Differentiation into
HPTC-like cells is induced on day 1, by supplementing the cultivation medium (REGM) with BMP2 and BMP7 (see also protocol by Kandasamy et al. (2015) in Fig. 3). HPTC-like cells are ready on day 8 for subsequent applications, like compound safety screening (Kandasamy et al. , 2015). Screening can be performed with the same multi-well plates used for cell seeding and differentiation, and no harvesting is required due to the high purity of hiPSCderived HPTC-like cells. The results for predicting human PTC toxicity can be determined on day 9 (Kandasamy et al. , 2015). A similar approach could be used for compound efficacy screening.
Figure 6: Platforms for the accurate prediction of compound-induced PTC toxicity in humans. Platforms that have been established already are illustrated at the top. Kandasamy et al. (2015) established a platform based on hiPSC-derived HPTC-like cells with a test balanced accuracy of $87\%$ (top, left; see also Fig. 5). Predictivity was determined based on compound-induced expression of IL6 and CXCL8 (determined by qPCR). The platform also correctly identified compound-induced molecular initiating events (MIE) and adverse outcome pathways (AOP) by using HCS and image analysis (Kandasamy et al. , 2015). In parallel, a predictive platform based solely on high-content screening (HCS) has been developed (Su et al. , 2015). This platform employed HPTC and HK-2 cells and has a test balanced accuracy of $82\%$ or $89\%$ , respectively, depending on the cell type. Image data analysis and result classification was performed with phenotypic profiling and machine learning. These technologies are currently integrated with the goal of developing an hiPSCbased HCS platform for the accurate prediction of PTC toxicity in humans (bottom). This platform should also be suitable for the determination of MIE and AOP.
Figure 7: Bioartificial kidneys with hiPSC-derived HPTC-like cells. A bioartificial kidney consists of a hemofilter replacing glomerular functions, which is used for normal hemofiltration. Hemofiltrate (yellow) and blood (the blood line is shown in red) are then directed into the bioreactor unit, which replaces PT functions. PTC are used for seeding the hollow fiber membranes (HFM) of the bioreactor unit. Use of hiPSC-derived PTC-like or HPTC-like cells (see Fig. 3) would be attractive for this application. The drawing shows a recently developed improved bioreactor design, where cells (green) reside on the outsides of the HFM (Oo et al. , 2013). Replacement fluid would be used in the same way as during normal hemofiltration, and a hemofiltrate reservoir would ensure approprite flow rates in the bioreactor unit. The urine-like fluid produced by the bioartificial kidney would flow into the waste reservoir (blue). Typically, commercial hemofiltration/hemodialysis cartridges are used for both units of a bioartificial kidney, and therefore both units would contain high numbers of HFM. Only two HFM are shown here in the bioreactor unit for illustration purposes.
Figure 8: Potential applications in regenerative medicine. For treatment of kidney patients, hiPSC-derived renal-like cells could be implanted (top, right) into the kidneys. Either renal-like progenitor cells, as generated by various protocols (Fig. 3, top), or more mature renal-like cell types could be used. Also structurally intact or dissociated progenitor structures or kidney organoids could be used (top, right). Alternatively, de-cellularized renal extracellular matrices could be implanted, after re-seeding with hiPSC-derived renal-like (progenitor) cells or dissociated progenitor structures / kidney organoids (bottom, right). Similarly derived cells could be also used for seeding of natural or synthetic polymeric matrices or for bioprinting of kidney-like organs (top, left) for implantation. Alternatively, constructs generated in these ways could be used for disease modeling or compound screening (bottom, left).
Figure 9: Kidney disease modelling. For renal disease modelling, hiPSC-derived renal-like cells or organoids could be used. PSC could be either genetically engineered in order to introduce mutations into genes that cause kidney disease.  Alternatively, hiPSC derived from kidney patients could be used.
